    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Barcelona grew from an Iberian settlement into the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a>, served as a royal seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and from 801 was the seat of the Counts of Barcelona, who became kings of the Crown of Aragon through a marriage in 1137.</li><li>After the Catalan institutions took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, its separate status was abolished in 1716, but the port, industry and the Eixample, laid out from the 1850s, made it one of the richest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>It was Republican in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, was bombed in March 1938 and fell on 26 January 1939, and under Franco the institutions of Catalonia were abolished; autonomy was restored in 1977.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 16 March 1794, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> were at war, the French astronomer Pierre François André Méchain was allowed into the fortress of Montjuïc to make observations used to measure the distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> to Barcelona, the basis of the first prototype metre.</p></aside>
    </section>

