    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 15 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> redraw the town as the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">415</span><span class="rf-text">The Visigothic king Ataulf holds his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> and is murdered there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">801</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, captures Barcelona after a siege of several months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">985</span><span class="rf-text">The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> sacks the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1162</span><span class="rf-text">Alfonso II becomes both Count of Barcelona and King of Aragon, following the marriage of 1137</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1714</span><span class="rf-text">The city falls after a siege and assault in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">Air raids from 16 to 18 March kill more than 1,000 people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1977</span><span class="rf-text">More than a million people demonstrate for the restoration of Catalan autonomy, which is granted within a month</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fratelli Alinari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Barcelona stands on a coastal plain about 5 km wide on the north-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, bounded by the Collserola range, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llobregat">Llobregat</a> river to the south-west and by the Besòs river to the north. The origin of the earliest settlement is unclear. Ruins of an early settlement in the El Raval neighbourhood, with tombs and dwellings, date to earlier than 5000 BCE. In the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE the area was settled by the Laietani, an Iberian people, at Barkeno on the Tàber hill, in the present Ciutat Vella, and at Laie, believed to have been on Montjuïc. Both struck coins that survive, and the name Barcelona comes from the Iberian Barkeno.</p><p>Historians have proposed founding stories since the 15th century. One credits the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, with founding the city around 230 BCE. The other credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules</a>, whose crew, after a ship was wrecked off the coast, founded a city called Barca Nona, the Ninth Ship. There is no evidence that Barcelona was ever a Carthaginian settlement or that its ancient name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> was connected with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a>, and the northern limit of Punic territory had been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, over 150 km to the south. A small Greek colony, Kallipolis, has been proposed nearby, but conclusive archaeological evidence has not been found.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> redrew the town about 15 BCE as a castrum centred on the Mons Taber. It was a colony, with a long official name beginning Colonia Faventia Julia Augusta Pia Barcino, which was shortened to Barcino around the end of the reign of Augustus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/via-augusta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-augusta.jpg" alt="Via Augusta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/via-augusta">Via Augusta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spain.Catalonia.Roda.de.Bara.Arc.Bera.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Year of the dragon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> was a small place. As a colony it was established to distribute land among retired soldiers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> listed it among the small settlements near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, which was far more important. Its position on a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-augusta">Via Augusta</a> allowed commercial development, and it enjoyed immunity from imperial taxation. In the 2nd century it had a population of 3,500 to 5,000, farmed the land around it and exported its wine widely. The forum's chief building was a temple of Augustus, 35 m by 17.5 m. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a>, in the later years of the 3rd century, a new double wall with seventy-eight towers was built, the strongest in the province of Tarraconensis, and Barcino grew in importance beside Tarraco.</p><p>Under the Counts of Barcelona the city prospered with the growing power of its overlords. In the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> it was by far the largest settlement of the Principality of Catalonia, at least four times larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>, and its economy turned increasingly to trade. In 1258 James I allowed its merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> to draw ordinances regulating maritime trade in the port, and in 1266 he allowed it to appoint consuls to all the major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports. The Taula de canvi, often viewed as the oldest public bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, was set up by the city magistrates in 1401.</p><p>In the 18th century the population grew from 30,000 to about 100,000 as the city became a key mercantile centre of the western Mediterranean. In the 19th century Catalan business interests, many of them based in Barcelona, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>: between 1817 and 1867 Catalans were involved in the transportation of 700,000 enslaved people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. In 1897 the city absorbed six surrounding municipalities. It had 533,000 people in 1900, and its population peaked in 1979 at 1,906,998.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaric-ii.jpg" alt="Alaric II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii">Alaric II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anneau_sigillaire_Alaric_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> contested Carthaginian control of the area, and Augustus declared the conquest of the peninsula complete in 19 BCE. In 415 the Visigothic king Ataulf established his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a>, where one of his own troops murdered him. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii">Alaric II</a> at Vouillé in 507 his successor Gesalec moved the Visigothic capital from Tolosa to Barcino, but Ibbas, an Ostrogothic general, defeated him outside the city in 512. Amalaric was murdered by his troops in Barcino, and his successor Theudis ruled from there until 548. The capital was fixed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 573.</p><p>Moorish forces entered the peninsula in 711, and after the devastation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> in 717 Barchinona surrendered peacefully and was spared major destruction. Muslim rule lasted roughly 85 years, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, took the city in 801 after a siege of several months. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> made the Spanish March a hereditary County of Barcelona in 865, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, appointed in 878, began a hereditary line on his death in 897. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila of Aragon</a> in 1137, and their son Alfonso II inherited the Crown of Aragon in 1162.</p><p>The marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> in 1469 united the two royal lines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> became the centre of political power. The Catalan institutions sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> against Philip V, and the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a>, in 1716, abolished the separate status of Catalonia. Barcelona was annexed to Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1812 to 1814.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="Diocletian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian,_from_Nikomedia,_Izmit,_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Christian communities in the Tarraconensis date from the 3rd century, and the community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> appears to have been established in the latter half of that century. Saint Cucuphas was martyred nearby under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, and Saint Eulalia is often considered a martyr of Barcino. The first recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Prætextatus, attended the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Sardica</a> in 343, and the first major church, the Basílica de la Santa Creu, was built about the end of the 4th century on the site of the later cathedral. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> formed only a minority of the population, held the positions of authority and were Arians until Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was adopted as the state religion in 589. Under Muslim rule the cathedral was converted into a mosque and taxes were levied on non-Muslims, though religious freedom was largely respected.</p><p>A Jewish population is dated from the mid-4th century at the latest. Under the Visigoths <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> suffered discrimination, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a>'s code of 654 outlawed many essential Jewish practices. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Barcelona had the largest Jewish community in the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, living in an area called the Call; Montjuïc, meaning Jewish Mountain, was the site of a medieval Jewish cemetery. The city was also the leading centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> of the Crown of Aragon until the 15th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> overtook it.</p><p>In the mid-1850s the density was 856 people per hectare, more than double that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the engineer Ildefons Cerdà proposed the Eixample, a grid of streets with wide avenues, gardens inside the blocks and octagonal blocks with chamfered corners.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 985 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> sacked Barcelona. The sack was so traumatic that most of the population was either killed or enslaved. The Massacre of 1391 diminished the Jewish community, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> later forced the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who refused to convert to burn at the stake or to sell their property and leave. In the late 17th and early 18th centuries the city repeatedly endured war: the bombing of 1691, the sieges of 1697, 1704, 1705 and 1706, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of 1713 and the siege and assault of 1714.</p><p>In the first half of the 19th century the people of Barcelona came into repeated conflict with the Spanish government in the revolts known as bullangues, from 1835 to 1843, which ended with the bombardment of 1842 and the Jamància insurrection of 1843. In the last week of July 1909, called Tragic Week, the working classes, backed by anarchists, socialists and republicans, rioted when reservists were mobilised to fight in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and over 100 citizens died.</p><p>In the Civil War many enterprises were collectivised by the CNT and UGT unions, and much of the city came under anarchist control until the street fighting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona-may-days">Barcelona May Days</a>. Aircraft bombed the city repeatedly, most severely from 16 to 18 March 1938, when more than 1,000 people died and over 2,000 were injured; the cathedral and parts of the Barri Gòtic were damaged. The city fell into Nationalist hands on 26 January 1939, and a mass exodus of civilians fled to the French border.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi.jpg" alt="Antoni Gaudí" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi">Antoni Gaudí</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoni_Gaudi_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pau Audouard Deglaire, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> survives in the layout of the historic centre, where the Roman grid plan is still visible in the Barri Gòtic, in fragments of the Roman walls built into the cathedral, and in the remains displayed underground at Plaça del Rei by the Museum of the History of Barcelona. The Eixample of Cerdà, imposed by the Spanish government after the city council had given the design of the extension to another architect, became the form of the modern city.</p><p>Catalan modernisme left an important legacy between 1885 and 1950. The work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi">Antoni Gaudí</a> includes the Sagrada Família, under construction since 1882 and financed by private donations; Park Güell, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> Güell and Casa Milà were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1984, with four more works added in 2005. The Palau de la Música Catalana and the Hospital de Sant Pau, by Lluís Domènech i Montaner, were inscribed in 1997. Mies van der Rohe's Barcelona Pavilion of 1929 was torn down in 1930, and a re-creation was built in 1986.</p><p>Under Franco the use of Catalan in public life was suppressed, and large-scale immigration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> followed. After the demonstrations of 11 September 1977 autonomy was restored, and the Olympic Games of 1992 brought the demolition of industrial buildings along the sea-front and the creation of 3 km of beach. Barcelona is now the capital of Catalonia and the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with about 1.7 million people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Barcelona, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, grew from an Iberian settlement into the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> and later the seat of the Counts of Barcelona. It became a great port of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and an industrial city, and is the capital of Catalonia.</em></p>

